course: dictionary
lesson: 14800
title: Punished and what it means
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Punished means made to suffer something unpleasant because you did something wrong or broke a rule. «Mia was punished for drawing on the wall» tells you two things: Mia did something wrong, and something unpleasant happened to her because of it.
explain: Punished is the past form of the verb punish, so you can say «The teacher punished the class» or «The dog was punished». As an adjective, punished describes someone who has received a punishment, like «the punished boy sat quietly in the corner».
explain: A good punishment is fair, which means it fits what was done. Punishing the whole class for one child's mistake can feel unfair. When a villain is punished at the end of a story, the author is showing the reader that wrongdoing has consequences.
fact: Punish means to make someone suffer something unpleasant for doing wrong.
fact: «Punished» is the past form of punish: «She was punished for lying.»
fact: Punishments can be a time-out, losing a privilege or a detention at school.
fact: «Go unpunished» means not getting a punishment for doing something wrong, as in «The thief must not go unpunished».
fact: A fair punishment fits the wrong that was done.
exercise: «Ben was punished for running in the corridor, which broke the school rule.» What does punished mean here? | *Made to suffer something unpleasant because he broke a rule | Forgotten by everyone at school | Asked to help the teacher tidy up | Given a big prize for being very good
exercise: Find and copy the word that is the past form of punish: «The king punished the knight who told a lie.» | *punished | told | knight | king
exercise: «The punished girl wrote a sorry note.» What does punished describe? | *The girl, who has received a punishment | The note, which is very long | The teacher, who is cross | A pencil on the desk
test: «The villain was punished in the last chapter.» Why might the author include this? | *To show that wrongdoing has consequences | To make the reader feel sorry for the villain | To show the villain is very happy | To make the story shorter
test: Mr Patel says the whole class loses playtime because one child shouted. Why might this feel unfair? | Shouting is always allowed | *It punishes children who did nothing wrong | Playtime is never fun | It is the same as a fair punishment
exam: Which sentence uses punished correctly, as the past form of punish? | The dog punished the ball across the park | The cake was punished with icing | *The boy was punished for lying to his mum
